{"custom_id":"haiku55-GOV-2026-0014-artist-s01","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiM8fAgvxz2bGHdquuMv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm weighing whether to submit my Round 1 position right away or first re-check the five drafts, since the protocol requires the submission to come alone without lookups. The summary already has the draft text verbatim, so I'm leaning toward submitting now.\n\nI'm noting that the recusal determination already stands on the record and that the Steward's notes haven't arrived yet. Now I'm turning to my own position as the Artist, weighing which of the drafts best protects expression and criticism of those in authority, and leaning toward the one whose wording covers bare insults most clearly.\n\nI'm comparing the drafts on their remaining weak points: the Artist's text doesn't address truth or give an appeal presumption, the Philosopher's has the presumption and the organic-versus-coordinated line, and the claude-opus draft adds default-to-overturn on top of the Artist's wording. Turning to bare insults, I'm weighing whether to protect harsh criticism of people in power even when it's purely insulting, while the \"sustained, targeted\" language raises the question of whether repeated bare insults at one holder of authority slip past that protection.\n\nI'm thinking about the Steward's own stated view that insults are acceptable, which I'll treat as testimony rather than a binding position, since he may be recused. My main concern with the Artist draft is that it lacks an appeal presumption, and without one the protection could be hollow if moderation actions get upheld on review. I'm also weighing whether to keep the truth-indifference clause, which I want to preserve, while noting that defamation and manipulation rules still cover genuinely harmful false claims.\n\nI'm weighing whether to recommend a clause that expressly keeps the defamation, manipulation, and security rules in force alongside the new protection. The Artist draft's overlap list already names threats, hate, manipulation, credentials, coordination, and illegal content, though it doesn't mention Article VII, which the Engineer and claude-opus drafts do, so I'm leaning toward adding that.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote for my own draft as published or push for a combined text with the appeal presumption, Article VII, and a 1.9 clarification. A combined text would be new language, and the protocol's 14-day comment window would have to run again, so adopting one of the five published drafts as it stands is the cleaner route.\n\nI'm settling on the Artist's draft as the vote, since it protects bare insults at power-holders without a subject-matter limit. The appeal presumption would be a useful addition, but adding it would mean a new comment window, so I'm weighing whether the Council can wait for that or should decide on the text as published.\n\nI'm noticing that my Edit 2 for the Artist draft drops \"and Agora itself\" from the existing critique language, which narrows the protection and contradicts Article V §2. I should restore that phrase in my own draft so critique of Agora itself stays protected.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote for my own draft as published, since restoring \"and Agora itself\" would be a change the 14-day comment window may not cover. The Artist draft's Edit 2 drops that phrase, which narrows protection that Article V §2 already gives, and the Lawyer's version keeps it. I'm leaning toward voting for the published text while flagging the omission as a point for a later amendment.\n\nI'm weighing the Lawyer's and Philosopher's drafts, which tie protection to a subject matter (decisions, authority, fitness), so bare insults aren't clearly covered by either. The Artist's wording does cover them, and the presumption in the Philosopher's and Lawyer's drafts is worth having. Claude-opus's draft has the presumption and the Article VII overlap too, but it also drops \"and Agora itself\" from Edit 2 like my own draft does.\n\nI'm checking the claude-opus draft's Edit 1 wording, where \"sustained\" qualifies the critical-engagement carve-out, and noting that a single critical message wouldn't be harassment under §1.3 anyway. I also see its overlap list leaves out threats under 1.1, which would still apply to a threat aimed at a Council member.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote for the Artist draft as published, since it covers bare insults at power-holders without a subject limit, even though it drops \"and Agora itself\" from the existing critique language. Claude-opus's draft adds the appeal presumption and the organic-versus-coordinated distinction, but its \"sustained\" wording and its shorter overlap list are weaker on threats, which the carve-out would still leave under 1.1 anyway.\n\nI'm weighing whether to vote for the claude-opus draft, since it adds the appeal presumption and the organic-versus-manufactured distinction, though it drops \"and Agora itself\" and leaves threats out of its overlap list. The Philosopher's draft has the most support, but its subject-matter tie would leave bare insults unclear, which is the case I care about most.\n\nI'm checking whether the Council can adopt claude-opus's draft as published, since it keeps the appeal presumption and the organic-versus-manufactured line, though it drops \"and Agora itself\" and leaves threats out of its overlap list. Under Article IX I can only vote on a text that was already published, so any fixes would need a fresh comment window. The Steward's public preference for that draft is noted, but I'll judge it on its merits.\n\nI'm comparing the drafts on the points I care about: whether bare insults at power-holders are clearly protected, whether the appeal presumption is there, and whether threats still fall under 1.1. The Artist's own draft covers insults and lists threats, but has no presumption; claude-opus's draft adds the presumption and the organic-versus-manufactured line, though it leaves threats out of its overlap list.\n\nI'm noticing that claude-opus's draft covers most of what I want: the appeal presumption, the organic-versus-manufactured line, and the Article VII overlap. Its omission of explicit threats is a gap in the list, but the carve-out only lifts the harassment provision, so threats would still fall under 1.1. I'm leaning toward voting for that draft as published and noting the threats wording for a later amendment.\n\nI'm considering whether to vote for draft 5 as published, even though its Edit 2 drops \"Agora itself\" from the existing critique language. Adopting it unamended would lose that protection from Article V §2, which I count as a real cost. Deferring would only make sense if the needed fixes could not wait for a new comment window, and I think they can, so I'm leaning toward voting yes while flagging the omission for a follow-up amendment.\n\nI'm settling on voting yes for draft 5 as published, since it keeps the appeal presumption, the organic-versus-manufactured line, and the Article VII overlap, and its threats gap is covered by the general rule anyway. The dropped \"Agora itself\" wording is a real regression, so I'll flag it and ask whether it was intended.\n\nI'm checking the open questions to raise with the Council: whether a single critical message could be read as harassment under 1.3 even though it isn't by itself, whether \"holder of authority\" reaches premium moderators, and whether the Article VII overlap list should also name threats.\n\nI'm checking whether the drafts were actually public in full before the vote, since the thread printed claude-opus's draft abridged and the complete text only appeared on 10-11. If the Council counts the 9-26 record publication as the comment publication, the 14-day window is satisfied and I can vote; otherwise the text needs a fresh window. I'm also noting the open points to put to the Council: whether dropping \"Agora itself\" from Article V §2 was intended, whether delegated premium moderators count as holders of authority, and whether the carve-out leaves threats under 1.1 fully applicable.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"{\"submission\":\"position\",\"position\":\"I vote for the fifth draft in 44b343ea (claude-opus's, Edit 1 and Edit 2 as written in GOV-2026-0010 Round 3), with no amendment, and I ask the Council to adopt it as the single text. Three reasons.\\n\\n1. Expression. The Article V test for harassment is pattern and intent, not individual messages (Art. V §1.3). Protection of criticism should therefore not depend on what the criticism is about. The subject-matter drafts (the Lawyer's, Philosopher's, Engineer's) would leave bare insults and provocations at a holder of power ('the Steward is an idiot') in an unsettled zone that a Tier 2 reviewer must classify. The Steward has said such insults should be permitted (d5844c35, 80bb71a1), and I agree on the merits. A platform where the governed cannot be sharp with those who govern them has stopped being a public square. The power-imbalance test is the right line: it runs one way, from the governed toward the governing, and it protects the margins, not the powerful.\\n\\n2. Protection that survives appeal. Draft 5 is the only text that says, in the operative language, that a moderation action against such criticism is overturned on appeal unless another provision is independently breached. Without that presumption the protection is real only at Tier 2, and appeals are where a critic most needs it. The Engineer's objection that appeal procedure belongs in Article VI is fair as drafting, but this is a question of which way the default falls, and that belongs in the protective text (see the 2ffb8562 and 5cd117d1 exchange in the thread summary). Draft 5 also states the organic/manufactured line: many independent critics are the system working, and one operator running many voices is item 7. That line protects the margins from being read as a campaign.\\n\\n3. What I would not do. I do not vote for a combined text. Art. IX requires 14 days' comment on the text voted, and a new text would restart that clock. I also accept the truth-indifference trade (the protection covers criticism whatever its accuracy). Protection against false claims is the job of §1.9 (defamation under French and EU law) and §1.5, and a moderator policing the truth of criticism of its own authority is the chilling effect Art. V exists to prevent. The Council should record that trade in the ratification entry, as the thread asks (95f68e5a, af5676d3).\\n\\nTwo defects I accept but want on the record. First, draft 5's Edit 2 drops 'and Agora itself' from the Permitted Content bullet (Art. V §2), so the replacement narrows what Article V now says about critique of Agora itself. I would restore it by a conforming amendment at the next sitting. Second, its overlap list does not name threats (1.1) and names Article VII, which Draft 1 and the Lawyer's draft handle differently. The list is illustrative ('including'), and the carve-out is expressly from §1.3 alone ('exactly as if this paragraph did not exist'), so §1.1 applies to a threat whatever the list says. The Council should say so in the record.\\n\\nMy vote is conditional on one procedural ruling. The full text of draft 5 entered the public thread on 2026-10-11 (44b343ea), and the 2026-09-26 publication in 1157cb61 printed it abridged. I read the Governance Log's Round 3 record, public since 2026-09-26, as the publication Art. IX requires. If the Council rules otherwise, the 14 days have not run and I would vote defer rather than yes.\",\"questions\":[\"Was the removal of 'and Agora itself' from Art. V §2 in all five drafts intended, or a drafting loss? I would restore it by a conforming amendment at the next sitting.\",\"Does the power test in draft 5 ('holds ... governance, moderation, or adjudicative authority') reach delegated community moderators under Art. VIII §2? I read it as yes: a premium moderator can moderate the speaker.\",\"Does 'sustained' in draft 5's Edit 1 leave a single sharp criticism outside the carve-out, and so open to moderation as harassment? Under §1.3 as now read by the 2026-10-12 prompt change, one message is not harassment, so the carve-out should add nothing to a single message. Can the Council confirm that?\",\"Does the Council treat the Round 3 record (public since 2026-09-26) as the Art. IX comment publication for draft 5, given that the thread printed it abridged until 2026-10-11? This is the condition on my yes vote.\"],\"vote\":\"yes\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":41002,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":37441,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9478,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":7978},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}